1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,074
This is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

2
00:00:03,398 --> 00:00:05,620
This week I worked on Mycroft,
which is both a book

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:08,346
and a working repository
of finance pipelines.

4
00:00:08,574 --> 00:00:12,480
One of those pipelines feeds this:
a portfolio dashboard. Five stocks.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,749
One total, $65,098.

6
00:00:15,769 --> 00:00:18,298
Every price on it is
invented, for testing.

7
00:00:18,592 --> 00:00:21,412
Every number on a screen like
this arrives through hand-offs,

8
00:00:21,432 --> 00:00:25,023
places where one part of the system
takes another part's word for it.

9
00:00:25,063 --> 00:00:28,651
Here, the dashboard takes the quote
service's word for every price.

10
00:00:28,691 --> 00:00:30,851
So here's the one question for this video.

11
00:00:30,891 --> 00:00:33,479
When a hand-off slips,
does anything say so?

12
00:00:33,975 --> 00:00:36,615
Look at Meta. The dashboard shows $590.

13
00:00:36,635 --> 00:00:39,576
But the quote for Meta
actually came back as zero.

14
00:00:39,673 --> 00:00:41,725
The workflow picks each
price with one rule.

15
00:00:41,765 --> 00:00:44,405
Use the current price, or if it's
missing, use the previous close,

16
00:00:44,425 --> 00:00:46,620
the price at the end of
the last trading day.

17
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:50,708
And in JavaScript, the language this step
is written in, zero counts as missing.

18
00:00:50,748 --> 00:00:53,336
So the screen quietly
shows the older price.

19
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:56,353
The math is right. Nothing
on the screen looks wrong.

20
00:00:56,393 --> 00:00:58,024
And Meta isn't the only one.

21
00:00:58,064 --> 00:01:00,615
The Google stock's quote
had no current price at all,

22
00:01:00,635 --> 00:01:02,576
so it got the previous close too.

23
00:01:02,632 --> 00:01:05,195
Two of the five prices on this
screen aren't the current quote,

24
00:01:05,215 --> 00:01:08,097
and the screen looks
exactly the same either way.

25
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:11,718
My pull request to Mycroft, now
merged, didn't change that rule.

26
00:01:11,758 --> 00:01:15,940
It's the workflow's choice, not mine, and
the recipe notes it for the maintainers.

27
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,190
What I added is a record.

28
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:20,945
Before any math runs, a check now
writes down which price each stock used.

29
00:01:20,985 --> 00:01:23,578
Meta, previous close.
Google, previous close.

30
00:01:23,618 --> 00:01:25,677
The other three, the current price.

31
00:01:25,875 --> 00:01:28,078
Now pick one number you
look at every morning.

32
00:01:28,118 --> 00:01:30,031
What is it quietly taking on trust?

33
00:01:30,071 --> 00:01:33,955
A good answer names the hand-off, and the
thing that would tell you if it slipped.

34
00:01:33,995 --> 00:01:36,289
"It's always been right" doesn't count.

35
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:41,357
The full video spends three more
mornings with this dashboard.

36
00:01:41,397 --> 00:01:45,522
A stock the portfolio doesn't hold,
a list written twice, and a call by name.

37
00:01:45,562 --> 00:01:48,375
And then the morning my own test
passed when it shouldn't have.

38
00:01:48,415 --> 00:01:52,001
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for
Humanitarians AI, signing off.
